.TH FENV 7 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fenv \- floating-point environment and exception handling
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The floating-point environment (fenv) provides functions and macros for
querying and controlling the state and exceptions of floating-point arithmetic.
It is defined by the C99 standard (7.12) and POSIX.1\-2001.

The fenv subsystem consists of:
.TP
.B fenv_t
A type representing the entire floating-point environment (rounding mode,
exception flags, and exception state).
.TP
.B feexcept_t
A type representing the floating-point exception flags.
.TP
.B FE_DFL_ENV
The default floating-point environment, pre\-initialized for the default
rounding mode (round to nearest) and no pending exceptions.

.SS Floating-Point Exceptions

The following macros represent individual floating-point exception flags.
They are bitwise\-representable; \fBfegetexceptflag\fR(3) and
\fBfesetexceptflag\fR(3) operate on the intersection of masks.

.TS
l l
c c
\fBMacro\fR\fB\t\fBCondition
\l\fBFE_DIVBY_ERR\tDivision by zero
\fBFE_INEXACT\tResult is not equal to the mathematical result
\fBFE_INVALID\tInvalid operation (NaN, undefined domain)
\fBFE_OVERFLOW\tResult too large to be represented
\fBFE_UNDERFLOW\tResult is small (subnormal or loss of precision)
\fBFE_OVERFLOW and FE_UNDERFLOW imply FE_INEXACT
.TE

.SS Rounding Modes

.TS
l l
c c
\fBMacro\fR\fB\t\fBDestination
\fBFE_TONEAREST\tRound to nearest (default)
\fBFE_DOWNWARD\tRound toward negative infinity
\fBFE_UPWARD\tRound toward positive infinity
\fBFE_TOWARDZERO\tRound toward zero
.TE

.SS x87/FPU vs SSE/MXCSR

On x87 (80-bit extended precision), the environment maps to the x87 control
word (0x37F default) and status word. On SSE2+, it maps to the MXCSR register
(0x1F80 default) and FPU status flags. The \fBFENV_ACCESS\fR pragma (if
supported) tells the compiler that the program inspects fenv state, preventing
optimizations that assume floating-point side effects are ignored.

.SS Usage Pattern

The typical usage pattern is:

1. Save the current environment with \fBfeholdexcept\fR(3) or
   \fBfegetenv\fR(3).
2. Perform floating-point operations.
3. Test for exceptions with \fBfetestexcept\fR(3).
4. Restore the saved state with \fBfeupdateenv\fR(3) or \fBfesetenv\fR(3).

.SS SEE ALSO

\fBfeclearexcept\fR(3), \fBfegetexceptflag\fR(3), \fBferaiseexcept\fR(3),
\fBfetestexcept\fR(3), \fBfegetround\fR(3), \fBfegetenv\fR(3),
\fBfeholdexcept\fR(3), \fBfeupdateenv\fR(3), \fBmath\fR(7)
